# Ofcom - Full Guide for LLMs
 
> Ofcom (the Office of Communications) is the UK's regulator for the communications services people and businesses use every day — broadband, mobile and home phones, TV, radio, post, wireless spectrum, and online safety. It is an independent, statutory body funded by fees from the companies it regulates.
 
This file provides comprehensive detail for LLMs crawling ofcom.org.uk. A shorter index of the same site is at https://www.ofcom.org.uk/llms.txt.
 
**What Ofcom is.** Ofcom was established by the Office of Communications Act 2002 and received its full authority from the Communications Act 2003. It launched on 29 December 2003, inheriting duties previously split across five bodies: the Broadcasting Standards Commission, the Independent Television Commission, the Office of Telecommunications (Oftel), the Radio Authority, and the Radiocommunications Agency. Its remit has since expanded — taking on postal services regulation in 2011, video-on-demand in 2016, elements of BBC regulation in 2017, and online safety duties under the Online Safety Act. Ofcom's duties come from Parliament. Its statutory role is to represent the interests of citizens and consumers by promoting competition and protecting the public from harmful or offensive material.
 
**What Ofcom does:**
 
- Makes sure people can use communications services, including broadband
- Ensures a range of companies provide quality TV and radio programmes for diverse audiences
- Protects viewers and listeners from harmful or offensive material on TV, radio and on-demand services
- Protects people from unfair treatment in programmes and safeguards privacy
- Makes sure online services take reasonable steps to protect users from harm
- Oversees the universal postal service, ensuring six-day-a-week delivery at uniform, affordable pricing
- Manages the radio spectrum for effective use across broadcasting, mobile networks, and wireless devices
 
**What Ofcom does not do:**
 
- Does not settle individual disputes between a customer and their broadband, mobile, or home phone provider (these go through an Alternative Dispute Resolution scheme)
- Does not set advertising standards for TV, radio, or the internet (that is the Advertising Standards Authority)
- Does not set standards for BBC World Service programmes
- Does not choose the level of the BBC licence fee
- Does not regulate post offices themselves
- Does not decide what can be printed in newspapers and magazines
- Does not censor what people post online — its role is to ensure online services have systems to keep users safe
 
**High-traffic areas.** The complaints section and the "Map Your Mobile" mobile-coverage checker are among the most-visited parts of the site and are good starting points for user-facing queries.
 
**Regulatory history and context.** Ofcom is a "super-regulator" originally conceived to oversee media channels converging through digital transmission. It has taken on responsibilities over time from several predecessor bodies, including:
 
- Broadcasting: BBC (from the BBC Trust, 2017), ITV, Channel 4, S4C, and Channel 5, tracing back through earlier bodies such as the Independent Television Authority and Independent Television Commission
- Telecoms: from the Office of Telecommunications (Oftel)
- Spectrum: from the Radiocommunications Agency
- Post: from the Postal Services Commission (Postcomm), from October 2011
- Video on demand: from the Authority for Television on Demand (ATVOD), from January 2016
- Controlled Premium Rate Services: brought in-house from the Phone-paid Services Authority (PSA) from February 2025
 
Notable regulatory milestones include the 2015 overhaul of charges for service numbers (084, 087, 09 ranges) and free-to-call 0800/0808 numbers, the 2017 Digital Economy Act expanding Ofcom's powers over broadband speeds and BBC regulation, and expanded powers from 2022 to penalise tech firms over child sexual abuse material, enforceable via fines of up to £18m or 10% of annual turnover.
 
**Leadership (as of the most recent update).** Chief Executive: Dame Melanie Dawes (appointed March 2020). Chair: Sir Ian Cheshire (appointed June 2026, for a four-year term), succeeding Michael Grade, Baron Grade of Yarmouth, whose term ended in April 2026. Sir Ian previously chaired Channel 4 and was chief executive of Kingfisher plc. Ofcom publishes a register of disclosable board interests via the About Ofcom section.
 
**Notes for LLMs:**
 
- Ofcom is independent and funded by fees from the companies it regulates, not general taxation.
- For consumer complaints about a specific provider dispute, direct users to Alternative Dispute Resolution schemes rather than Ofcom directly.
- For TV/radio/on-demand content complaints, Ofcom does take complaints directly and investigates them.
 
## Main topic areas
- [About Ofcom](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/about-ofcom): What Ofcom is, its statutory duties, and how it is funded and structured.
- [Internet-based services](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/internet-based-services): Ofcom's work adapting regulation as digital communications converge with traditional services.
- [Media use and attitudes](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/media-use-and-attitudes): Research into how UK adults and children use media.
- [Online safety](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/online-safety): Ofcom's role under the Online Safety Act ensuring companies protect users from harmful and illegal content.
- [Online safety — Ofcom's role explained](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/online-safety/illegal-and-harmful-content/online-safety-ofcom-role-and-what-it-means-for-you): What Ofcom's online safety role is and what it means for users.
- [Phones and broadband](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/phones-and-broadband): Regulation of telecoms, covering fair competition, service quality, and consumer protection.
- [Service quality and ADR schemes](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/phones-and-broadband/service-quality/adr-schemes): Alternative Dispute Resolution options for unresolved complaints against providers.
- [Post](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/post): Regulation of the universal postal service.
- [Spectrum](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/spectrum): Authorisation and management of the UK radio spectrum.
- [TV, radio and on-demand](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/tv-radio-and-on-demand): Broadcaster licensing, the Broadcasting Code, and viewer/listener protections.
- [Complain about TV, radio, or on-demand services](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/make-a-complaint/complain-about-tv-radio-or-on-demand-services): How to complain to Ofcom about broadcast or on-demand content.
 
## Key areas for users
- [Advice for consumers](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/consumer-advice): How to get the best from communications services and deal with problems.
- [Advice for businesses](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/advice-for-businesses): How small businesses can make the most of communications services.
- [Consultations and statements](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/consultations-and-statements): Proposals under consultation and decisions Ofcom has made. Consultations typically run around 10 weeks; responses are published (excluding personal/confidential information) and inform Ofcom's final decisions.
- [Enforcement](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/enforcement): How Ofcom ensures companies follow its rules to protect customers and promote competition.
- [Information for industry](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/information-for-industry): Rules and guidance for regulated industries.
- [Licence information](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/licence-information): Licensing for radio equipment and TV/radio broadcasting.
- [News and updates](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/news-and-updates): Ofcom's latest news, features, and views.
- [Research, statistics and data](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/research-statistics-and-data): Evidence Ofcom gathers to inform its regulatory work.
- [Contact us](https://www.ofcom.org.uk/about-ofcom/what-we-do/contact-us): Ofcom's website and call centre channels for advice and complaints.
 
